  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🎉 Event Planning Business Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for an event planner or party planning business, from Save the Date Events to Maison Fête."
    },
    "intro": "Good event planning names say what kind of events you plan and how they'll feel: <em>Planned to Perfection</em> for organised calm, <em>Glitter &amp; Lemonade</em> for children's parties, <em>Gold &amp; Linen Event Design</em> for weddings with a budget. Name your planning business with this **Event Planning Business Name Generator**: for event planners, party planners and event management companies. Pick a style, or add your first name or town. To name an event itself, try the <a href=\"event-name-generator.html\">Event Name Generator</a>.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good event planning business names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Planned to Perfection</strong>: says exactly what clients are paying for.</li><li><strong>Occasionally Yours</strong>: a gentle pun on a classic letter sign-off.</li><li><strong>Fanfare Event Co.</strong>: short, bright and easy to say on the phone.</li><li><strong>Glitter &amp; Lemonade</strong>: two things from every good children's party.</li><li><strong>Firefly &amp; Candles</strong>: a soft, cute pairing that suits garden parties and weddings.</li><li><strong>Gold &amp; Linen Event Design</strong>: a luxury name: materials, not jokes.</li><li><strong>Mingle &amp; Co.</strong>: modern and short, with room to grow.</li><li><strong>Sarah Rose Events &amp; Parties</strong>: your own name, with both kinds of work in it.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What event planning names say about you",
        "content": "<p>Couples, parents and companies hire planners for very different things, and the name is how they tell you apart before they read your website.</p><ul><li><strong>Weddings and premium events</strong> suit calm, polished names: <em>Event Design</em>, <em>Occasions</em> or <em>Weddings</em>, with a material or colour like <em>linen</em>, <em>pearl</em> or <em>gold</em>. <em>The Pearl Table</em> sounds like someone who folds napkins properly.</li><li><strong>Children's and family parties</strong> suit cheerful names with a party word in them: <em>Tiny Sparkler Party Planning</em>, <em>Ribbon &amp; Fizz</em>.</li><li><strong>Corporate events</strong> suit plain, confident names: a surname or place plus <em>Events</em>, like <em>Everett Events</em>.</li><li><strong>A mix of everything</strong> suits a broad word like <em>Celebrations</em> or <em>Event Co.</em>, so you don't turn work away because of your name.</li></ul><p>Short names fit an Instagram handle and a business card, and they're easier to say when you're answering the phone at a venue.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>Every name here has a <strong>Check</strong> button, which opens the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>: it looks up domains, social handles, the company register and trade mark searches in one place. Before you order signs or cards:</p><ul><li><strong>Company register.</strong> In the UK, search Companies House; a limited company's name can't be the same as one already registered. In the US, search your state's business register.</li><li><strong>Trade marks.</strong> Search the UK Intellectual Property Office or the USPTO for marks in your line of work. Registering a company doesn't give you trade mark rights.</li><li><strong>Domain and handles.</strong> Try the .co.uk or .com and the Instagram and Facebook names at the same time, and change the name slightly rather than settle for a messy handle.</li><li><strong>Local search.</strong> Look up the name with your town. Two similar businesses a few miles apart confuse customers and reviews.</li></ul><p>This is general guidance, not legal advice. For the full process, read <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>.</p><p>To name the events themselves, try the <a href=\"event-name-generator.html\">Event Name Generator</a>. If you photograph them too, there's the <a href=\"photography-business-name-generator.html\">Photography Business Name Generator</a>, and for a business name in your own words, the <a href=\"business-name-generator.html\">Business Name Generator</a>.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_services-names.html\">professional and creative business name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What's a good name for an event planning business?",
        "answer": "One that says what you plan and suits your clients. \"Events\", \"Event Co.\" and \"Celebrations\" fit most planners; \"Party Planning\" says children's and family parties; \"Event Design\" and \"Occasions\" sound premium. Keep it short enough for a business card and an Instagram handle, and avoid tying it to one type of event if you want to grow."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I call a party planning business?",
        "answer": "Something cheerful with a party word in it, like Glitter & Lemonade or Tiny Sparkler Party Planning. Choose the Cute or Catchy style for names like these."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should I name my event business after myself?",
        "answer": "It works well if clients are booking you personally, as many wedding planners find: Sarah Rose Events tells them who they'll be working with. A name that isn't yours is easier to grow into a team or sell later."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I check an event planning business name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. In the UK, search Companies House; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Check your local area too, as two similar names nearby confuse customers."
      }
    ]
  },
